From 393949db0f6bde07598502e2dcee927d45371133 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:51:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: native Dolby Vision Low-Latency where the
 display offers no other

A TV input that offers Dolby Vision only as Low-Latency (the LG C5 in its game mode, a TV without the
Standard mode) played it as HDR10, with a notification that told the user to leave the game mode.
With "Dolby Vision Low-Latency (player-led)" turned on (System > Display,
videoscreen.hadesdvlowlatency, off by default), native output sends it as Low-Latency:

- only where the display offers Low-Latency and not Standard
  (CDisplayInfo::SupportsLowLatencyDolbyVisionOnly()): a display that takes Standard keeps it, whatever
  the setting says, and nothing of its output changes;
- only where the kernel or the module says it can (DVBRIDGE_DV_LOW_LATENCY_LAB, linux-9975 to 9977;
  NV_HDMI_DV_LOW_LATENCY, nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9030); else the reason is "lldriver", and no other
  signal is sent instead;
- the output is captured as for Standard, with that property set and, on NVIDIA, the Dolby VSIF with
  low_latency (46 d0 00 03, the bytes the kernel packs for it); the RGB range selection of the sink
  (VCDB QS) is not asked for, as the AVI InfoFrame says YCbCr in the limited range;
- the picture is mapped to the display's Low-Latency target (TakeDVLowLatencyMapping(): the peak and the
  primaries of its Dolby VSVDB, white D65; the gamut of the mastering display where the picture's HDR10
  metadata gives it), and sent without metadata (CDVBridgeGLES::SetLowLatency());
- Dolby Vision at 2160p50 and 60 by the Low-Latency mode of the block;
- the player information says "Low-Latency, mapped to the display by LibreELEC Hades", and why it is
  not used ("lloff": the setting is off; "lldriver"); the log says how the picture is mapped;
- the notification for such a display says that Low-Latency can be turned on, where the driver can
  send it;
- <video><dvllpattern>true</dvllpattern> in advancedsettings.xml shows a test pattern in place of the
  picture, for the first look at a display: the bars in their colours with white and black neutral, a
  grey ramp, and luma and chroma staircases that must be smooth.

The mapping is LibreELEC Hades', not Dolby's display management, which is not public: the setting
says so. Strings in English, with es_ES, es_AR and es_MX in Estuary (core #39870 to #39872, Estuary
