@@ -25357,7 +25357,28 @@ msgctxt "#39851"
 msgid "8-bit RGB"
 msgstr ""
 
-#empty strings from id 39852 to 39889
+#empty strings from id 39852 to 39869
+
+#. Hades: label of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvlowlatency"; translated in Estuary (#31920)
+#: system/settings/settings.xml
+msgctxt "#39870"
+msgid "Dolby Vision Low-Latency (player-led)"
+msgstr ""
+
+#. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvlowlatency"; translated in Estuary (#31921)
+#: system/settings/settings.xml
+msgctxt "#39871"
+msgid "On a TV input that offers Dolby Vision only as Low-Latency (its game mode, or a TV that has no other), sends Dolby Vision that way: LibreELEC Hades maps the picture to the display itself, as a player does for Low-Latency, with the BT.2390 curve guided by the level 1 metadata of each scene, to the peak and the colours that the display gives for it. The mapping is that of LibreELEC Hades, not that of Dolby. A display that takes Dolby Vision Standard keeps it, whatever this says. Applies from the next video."
+msgstr ""
+
+#. Hades: notification for a Dolby Vision video on a TV input that offers Low-Latency only, where the
+#. output driver can send it; translated in Estuary (#31922)
+#: xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbm.cpp
+msgctxt "#39872"
+msgid "This TV input offers Dolby Vision only in its low-latency form, so Dolby Vision plays as on a TV without it. Turning on Dolby Vision Low-Latency (Settings > System > Display) sends it in that form. If game settings on this input cause it (LG: Quick Frame Transport), turn them off for native Dolby Vision; some TVs offer no other form."
+msgstr ""
+
+#empty strings from id 39873 to 39889
 
